    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-130807",
      "knowledge_point_id": "dd12bc7b-1b5d-5af2-bcef-c9d0fda7d8fc",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing a Product Involving the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix",
      "problem": "Find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-8){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}49 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
